object CodePointRepository {
    // Stored as chunked string literals rather than one intArrayOf(...) of
    // every character's code point -- see buildKotlinSource in lawgpt.html
    // for why.
    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 237 substantial performance[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent (Cardozo,\nJ.), as restated by the casebook in Note 4, citing Restatement\n(Second) of Contracts § 237\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Each party's duty to perform is implicitly\nconditioned on there being no uncured material failure of\nperformance by the other party; minor or immaterial deviations\nare not a failure of a condition to the other party's duty,\nthough they still give the other party a right to damages\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When does one party's incomplete or defective\nperformance justify the other party in refusing to render its\nown performance?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The performing party rendered performance that departs\n    from the contract only in an insignificant or immaterial way\n    2. The departure does not \"in any real or substantial\n    measure\" frustrate the purpose of the contract\n    3. Under Cardozo's version, the omission is \"both trivial\n    and innocent,\" meaning not willful (but see Restatement\n    (Second) § 241(e) below)\n    4. The performing party \"offer[s] atonement,\" meaning the\n    other party gets an allowance for the resulting damage\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Usually raised by a party who performed\nimperfectly and is suing to enforce the other side's return\nperformance (in Jacob & Youngs, a builder suing for the unpaid\nbalance); if performance was substantial, the builder recovers\nthe contract price minus damages for the defects; if not, the\nother party's duty to perform never arises\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m:\n    - The parties may \"by apt and certain words\" make full\n    performance of every term an express condition of recovery;\n    Cardozo said that question was \"not here\"\n    - Under Cardozo's opinion a \"willful transgressor\" cannot\n    use the doctrine\n    - There is no \"general license to install whatever, in the\n    builder's judgment, may be regarded as 'just as good'\"\n    - A change that is \"so dominant or pervasive\" that it\n    frustrates the contract's purpose is never tolerated\n    - The doctrine applies to all types of agreements, not just\n    construction contracts (Note 5: Nature's Plus Nordic A/S v.\n    Nat. Organics, Inc.; Hull v. Giesler; Viacom Int'l Inc. v.\n    MGA Ent., Inc.)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Substantial performance answers whether the other\nparty must still perform; it does not answer how much the\ndefects cost, which is the separate damages question (see the\nmeasure-of-damages rule below)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Dependent and independent promises[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent, citing\nAnson on Contracts (Corbin's ed.) § 367 and 2 Williston on\nContracts §§ 841-842\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Some promises are so plainly independent that they\ncan never be conditions of one another, some are so plainly\ndependent that they must always be conditions, and others are\n\"dependent and thus conditions when there is departure in point\nof substance\" but \"will be viewed as independent and collateral\nwhen the departure is insignificant\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Is one party's promised performance a\ncondition of the other party's duty, so that a failure to\nperform excuses the other side, or only a promise whose breach\ngives rise to damages?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Classification depends on \"considerations partly\nof justice and partly of presumable intention,\" weighing:\n    1. \"The purpose to be served\"\n    2. \"The desire to be gratified\"\n    3. \"The excuse for deviation from the letter\"\n    4. \"The cruelty of enforced adherence\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Courts use it to decide whether a deviation\ntriggers forfeiture (the promise is treated as a condition) or\nonly damages (it is treated as independent); relevant factors\ninclude:\n    - Simple, uniform transactions versus complex ones\n    - Whether the subject matter can be returned (chattels) or\n    cannot be surrendered because it is \"united to the land\"\n    (buildings)\n    - Whether the field is art or \"mere utility\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The law \"will be slow to impute\" a purpose to\nrequire literal fulfillment \"in the silence of the parties,\nwhere the significance of the default is grievously out of\nproportion to the oppression of the forfeiture\"; clear contract\nlanguage can override this presumption\n\n[0m[1mRule: Materiality as a question of degree[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent, citing\nCrouch v. Gutmann, Woodward v. Fuller, and Easthampton L. & C.\nCo. v. Worthington\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Where the line falls between the important and the\ntrivial \"cannot be settled by a formula\"; it is \"a question of\ndegree,\" decided by the triers of fact if there is doubt and by\nthe judges as a matter of law if the inferences are certain\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Who decides whether a deviation is\nsubstantial, and by what method?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A deviation from the contract's terms\n    2. A case-specific, contextual assessment of its\n    significance, because \"the same omission may take on one\n    aspect or another according to its setting\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is why the trial court's exclusion of\nevidence in Jacob & Youngs was error; evidence that the pipe was\nequivalent bore on a factual question of degree, which a\ndirected verdict improperly cut off\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Where the inferences are certain, the court may\ndecide materiality as a matter of law (for example, Nature's\nPlus, where 99.5 percent compliance was substantial performance\nas a matter of law)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Willful transgressor bar[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent, citing\nSchultze v. Goodstein and Desmond-Dunne Co. v. Friedman-Doscher\nCo.\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"The willful transgressor must accept the penalty\nof his transgression\"; for him \"there is no occasion to mitigate\nthe rigor of implied conditions,\" while \"the transgressor whose\ndefault is unintentional and trivial may hope for mercy if he\nwill offer atonement for his wrong\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can a party whose deviation was intentional\ninvoke substantial performance?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A deviation from the contract\n    2. The deviation was willful rather than due to oversight or\n    inattention\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Cardozo stressed that the trial evidence\nsupported a finding that the omission was \"neither fraudulent\nnor willful\" but resulted from the subcontractor's \"oversight\nand inattention\"; the dissent argued the failure was \"either\nintentional or due to gross neglect,\" which \"amounted to the\nsame thing\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Professor Corbin criticized willfulness as a vague\nconcept, and the Restatement (Second) rejects an automatic bar\n(see the next rule)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 241(e)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 241(e) and Comment\nf, as described in Note 7, adopting the view in 3A Corbin on\nContracts § 707\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A willful breach does not automatically bar\nrecovery, but the motive of the breaching party is a factor in\ndetermining whether performance was substantial\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How does the breaching party's state of mind\naffect whether its performance was substantial?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The breaching party's motive is weighed as one\nfactor in the materiality analysis rather than treated as a\nthreshold bar\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This replaces Cardozo's categorical \"willful\ntransgressor\" rule with a balancing approach; on an exam,\ncontrast it with Jacob & Youngs and Roudis v. Hubbard\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Some courts still apply a stricter rule; see First\nNat'l Bank of Omaha v. Centennial Park, LLC (substantial\nperformance will not apply when the breach is a willful,\nintentional deviation from the contract terms) and Roudis v.\nHubbard\n\n[0m[1mRule: Measure of damages for defective performance[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent, citing\nSpence v. Ham and Handy v. Bliss\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: In most cases the cost of replacement is the\nmeasure, because the owner is entitled to the money that will\nlet him complete, \"unless the cost of completion is grossly and\nunfairly out of proportion to the good to be attained,\" in which\ncase \"the measure is the difference in value\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When a party has substantially but imperfectly\nperformed, what allowance must be deducted from the contract\nprice for the defects?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Default rule: cost of completion or replacement\n    2. Switch to diminution in value when the cost of completion\n    is grossly and unfairly disproportionate to the benefit\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: In Jacob & Youngs, replacing the pipe meant\ndemolishing substantial parts of the house, so the allowance was\nthe difference in value, which \"would be either nominal or\nnothing\"; Cardozo's Vermont-versus-New-Hampshire granite\nhypothetical illustrates the same point; the measure \"must be\nshaped to the same end\" of justice as the substantial\nperformance doctrine itself\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m:\n    - Cardozo noted some exposed sections \"might perhaps have\n    been replaced at moderate expense,\" but the owner demanded\n    replacement of the plumbing \"as a unit\" from cellar to roof\n    - Roudis v. Hubbard limits the diminution-in-value measure\n    to unintentional breaches\n    - The casebook says it will return to this issue in Chapter\n    10 (expectation damages), which the attached material does\n    not cover\n\n[0m[1mRule: Diminution in value limited to good-faith breach[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Roudis v. Hubbard, 574 N.Y.S.2d 95\n(App. Div. 1991), as described in Note 7, relying on Jacob &\nYoungs\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Even if a contractor has substantially performed, a\ndiminution-in-value measure should be applied only where the\ncontractor's breach was unintentional and constituted\nsubstantial performance in good faith; otherwise the measure is\nthe cost of completion\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can a builder who intentionally omits required\nwork limit the owner to market-value damages?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The breach was unintentional\n    2. The breach constituted substantial performance in good\n    faith\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The builder intentionally left out the footing\ndrains and insulation because he thought them \"unnecessary,\" so\nthe owners recovered the cost of completion even though the\nhouse's value was not diminished, and even though the contract\nmade market-value damages the sole remedy upon substantial\ncompliance\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The attached material does not describe an\nexception\n\n[0m[1mRule: Substantial performance standard[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Thomas Haverty Co. v. Jones, 197 P.\n105 (Cal. 1921), quoted in Note 5; Flynn Builders, L.C. v.\nLande, 814 N.W.2d 542 (Iowa 2012)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: There is substantial performance where the variance\nfrom the specifications \"does not impair the building or\nstructure as a whole,\" and the building is actually used for its\nintended purpose, or the defects can be remedied \"without great\nexpenditure and without material damage to other parts of the\nstructure\"; the defects must not \"run through the whole work\" or\nbe so substantial that the allowance out of the price \"will not\ngive the owner essentially what he contracted for\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When is performance \"substantial\"?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The variance does not impair the structure as a whole,\n    and\n    2. Either the building is actually used for its intended\n    purpose, or the defects can be remedied without great\n    expense and without material damage to other parts of the\n    structure\n    3. The defects do not run through the whole work so that the\n    owner's object of having the work done a particular way is\n    defeated\n    4. A price allowance will still give the owner essentially\n    what he bargained for\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Flynn Builders held that a contractor who\ncompleted about 80 to 85 percent of a roughly \$260,000 house had\nnot substantially performed, because unfinished work estimated\nat 5 to 7 weeks (doors, cabinets, drywall, flooring, painting,\nconcrete, HVAC) \"materially affected the habitability of the\nhouse\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: No mathematical percentage rule can be stated\n(Flynn Builders); percentages near full compliance can establish\nsubstantial performance (99.5 percent in Nature's Plus; at least\n96 percent in Viacom)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Material breach defined by fundamental purpose[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Hull v. Giesler, 331 P.3d 507 (Idaho\n2014), described in Note 5\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A material breach is one that \"touches fundamental\npurpose of contract\" and \"defeats the purpose of the parties\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What makes a breach material rather than\nimmaterial?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The breach must go to the contract's fundamental\npurpose and defeat the parties' purpose\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A buyer who paid the installments due over five\nyears for a one-half interest in land substantially performed\neven though some payments were late\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The attached material does not describe an\nexception\n\n[0m[1mRule: Strict performance view[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Dissent in Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent (McLaughlin,\nJ.), relying on Smith v. Brady, 17 N.Y. 173\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The owner \"had a right to contract for what he\nwanted\" and to get it before paying, whatever his reason, even\n\"a mere whim\"; a builder who departs from the specifications\nwithout waiver cannot claim he built as good a building, and if\nthe conditions of payment are unmet, \"the right to demand it\ndoes not exist\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Must a builder strictly comply with the\nspecifications before the owner's duty to pay arises?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The contract specifies a particular performance\n    2. The builder departs from it\n    3. The owner has not waived performance\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The dissent would have affirmed the directed\nverdict for Kent; it argued that substantial performance\n\"depends in no small degree upon the good faith of the\ncontractor\" and has no application where only about two-fifths\nof the pipe conformed and no explanation was given\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Even the dissent accepts substantial performance\nfor \"minor omissions, due to inadvertence,\" with recovery of the\nprice less full compensation for those omissions; Note 4 asks\nwhether the dissent disagrees with the doctrine itself or only\nwith how it was applied\n\n[0m[1mRule: Express condition of architect's certificate[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook narrative, Note 3, citing Nolan v. Whitney,\n88 N.Y. 648 (1882), Glenn Constr. Co. v. Bell Aerospace Servs.,\nInc., and Town of Plainfield v. Paden Eng'g Co.\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Under the majority view, if the contractor's right\nto payment is expressly conditioned on the architect's\ncertificate, the condition is strictly enforced and the\ncontractor is denied recovery unless it shows a ground for\nexcusing the condition, such as fraud, bad faith, or waiver;\nunder New York law (Nolan v. Whitney), an architect cannot\nproperly refuse a certificate if the contractor has\nsubstantially performed\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What happens when payment is expressly\nconditioned on an architect's certificate that is withheld?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. An express contractual condition of payment upon the\n    architect's certificate\n    2. Non-issuance of the certificate\n    3. For the contractor to recover in a majority jurisdiction,\n    an excuse such as fraud, bad faith, or waiver\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Article IX of the Kent contract provided for\npayment \"only upon certificates of the Architect,\" so Jacob &\nYoungs could have been treated as an express-condition case; in\nNew York the result would probably have been the same, but in\nother jurisdictions it might differ\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: New York's rule in Nolan v. Whitney departs from\nthe majority view\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restitution for a materially breaching party[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook narrative, Note 8, citing Kreyer v. Driscoll,\n159 N.W.2d 680 (Wis. 1968)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Many courts allow a contractor that has committed a\nmaterial breach to recover in restitution (quantum meruit) for\nthe reasonable value of its services, less any harm resulting\nfrom the breach\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can a contractor who did not substantially\nperform recover anything?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The contractor did not substantially perform\n    2. The contractor conferred services with a reasonable value\n    3. That value is reduced by any harm caused by the breach\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A fallback theory when the substantial\nperformance claim on the contract fails\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading says \"many courts\" allow it, which\nimplies not all do\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 240 divisibility[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 240 and Comment e,\nas described in Note 8; Carrig v. Gilbert-Varker Corp., 50\nN.E.2d 59 (Mass. 1943)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If a contract is divisible, a court may allow\nrecovery for the portions that have been completed\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can a party who has not fully performed\nrecover for completed parts of the contract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. It must be possible to apportion the parties'\n    performances into corresponding pairs of part performances\n    2. It must be proper to treat those pairs as \"agreed\n    equivalents\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: In Carrig, a contract to build 35 houses in\ngroups of 10 was divisible, so a contractor who completed 20\nhouses could recover\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Under Comment e, the agreed-equivalents\nrequirement protects the paying party; he should not have to pay\nthe same fraction of the price for a fraction of the performance\n\"unless it appears that the performance that he actually\nreceived is worth to him roughly that same fraction of what full\nperformance would have been worth to him\"\n\n[0m[1mRule: Recovery by a substantially performing contractor[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Lewis Elec. Co. v. Miller, 791 N.W.2d\n691 (Iowa 2010), described in Note 6\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A contractor who substantially performed but whose\nwork was defective may recover the remaining contract price less\nthe owner's cost to complete or repair, and the owner gets\njudgment for the excess if repair costs exceed the unpaid\nbalance\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How is the money settled when performance is\nsubstantial but defective?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Substantial performance\n    2. Defective work\n    3. Repair or completion costs actually incurred by the owner\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This illustrates that substantial performance\ndoes not end the inquiry; the defects still have to be paid for\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Cost of repair gives way to diminution in value\nwhen it is grossly disproportionate (Jacob & Youngs)\n\n[0m[1mRule: Prior nonpayment excusing the contractor's performance[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Remand ruling in Flynn Builders, L.C.",
        " v. Lande,\naffirmed by the Iowa Court of Appeals, 844 N.W.2d 469 (2014),\ndescribed in Note 5\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A contractor's failure to render substantial\nperformance may be excused by the owner's earlier failure to\nmake payments when due\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can the owner's own earlier breach excuse the\ncontractor's failure to substantially perform?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The owner failed to make payments when due\n    2. That failure came before the contractor's failure to\n    substantially perform\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Even though the Iowa Supreme Court held Flynn\nBuilders had not substantially performed, on remand its failure\nwas excused by the owner's earlier nonpayment\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The attached material does not describe an\nexception\n\n[0m[1mRule: Independent covenants[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Casebook Comment on the doctrine of constructive\nconditions, describing Nichols v. Raynbred, 80 Eng. Rep. 238\n(K.B. 1615)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Under the early seventeenth-century English rule,\nmutual promises in bilateral contracts were \"independent,\" so\neven if one party failed to perform, the other was not justified\nin refusing to perform and was left to bring a separate action\nfor breach\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can one party's nonperformance be raised as a\ndefense to the other party's suit on the contract?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Mutual promises in a bilateral contract\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A seller suing for the price of a cow did not\nhave to plead that the cow had been delivered; the buyer's\nremedy was an independent action against the seller\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m:\n    - McGovern (52 Tul. L. Rev. 659) argues that English courts\n    actually held promises dependent in many types of contracts\n    - The conventional view is that the rule was displaced by\n    Kingston v. Preston\n\n[0m[1mRule: Mansfield's three kinds of covenants[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Kingston v. Preston, reported in Jones\nv. Barkley, 99 Eng. Rep. 437 (K.B. 1773), quoted in the casebook\nComment\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Covenants are either:\n1. Mutual and independent, where each party may recover damages\nfor breach and the other's breach is no excuse\n2. Conditions and dependent, where one performance depends on\nprior performance of the other\n3. Mutual conditions to be performed at the same time, where a\nparty who was ready and offered to perform may sue the one who\nneglected or refused\nDependency \"was to be collected from the evident sense and\nmeaning of the parties\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How do courts decide whether one party's\nperformance must come before, or alongside, the other's?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Interpretation of the parties' evident sense and\nmeaning, including the \"essence of the agreement\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Because the essence of the deal was that the\nseller would not rely on the buyer's personal credit, the\nbuyer's giving of security was a condition precedent to the\nseller's duty to hand over the business\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The parties' evident intent controls the\nclassification\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 234(1) concurrent conditions[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding in Morton v. Lamb, 101 Eng. Rep. 890\n(K.B. 1797); Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 234(1) and\n238, described in the casebook Comment\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: When the two performances can be rendered at the\nsame time, they are presumed due simultaneously, absent\nagreement otherwise, so neither party has to extend credit to\nthe other; each party's readiness to perform is a \"concurrent\ncondition\" of the other's duty, and a party suing for\nnonperformance must show that she performed or at least tendered\nperformance\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Absent agreement, in what order are\nperformances due when they can be exchanged at the same time?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The performances are capable of simultaneous rendition\n    2. The agreement does not provide otherwise\n    3. To sue, the plaintiff must allege and show performance or\n    readiness and tender of performance\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Contracts to convey land and to sell goods are\ntreated as capable of simultaneous performance, so a buyer or\nseller must show at least a tender to sue the other side for\nbreach\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: This is a rule of construction only and gives way\nto contrary agreement\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 234(2) longer performance first[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Morton v. Lamb; Restatement (Second) of Contracts §\n234(2), described in the casebook Comment\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: If the performances cannot be rendered at the same\ntime, the performance requiring the longer period of time must\nbe rendered before the performance requiring the shorter period\nof time is due\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Who must perform first when the performances\ncannot be exchanged simultaneously?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The performances cannot be rendered at the same time\n    2. One performance takes a longer period than the other\n    3. The agreement does not provide otherwise\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Construction and employment contracts are\nordinarily construed to require the work to be completed before\npayment is due; this is why the builder in Jacob & Youngs had to\nshow substantial performance before Kent's duty to pay the\nbalance arose\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: This is merely a rule of construction; such\ncontracts ordinarily call for payment at stated intervals as the\nwork progresses\n\n[0m[1mRule: UCC § 2-507 and § 2-511 tender as condition[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: UCC §§ 2-507 and 2-511 and Comment 1 to § 2-511,\ndescribed in the casebook Comment\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Tender of delivery is a condition of the buyer's\nduty to accept and pay for goods (§ 2-507), and tender of\npayment is a condition of the seller's duty to deliver (§ 2-511)\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: In a sale of goods, what must each party do\nbefore the other party's duty arises?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A contract for the sale of goods\n    2. Tender by the party seeking the other's performance\n    3. No agreement to the contrary\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: These provisions mirror the Restatement's\nconcurrent-conditions rule for goods\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: These are rules of construction only; Comment 1 to\n§ 2-511 notes that the payment-against-delivery requirement has\n\"no application\" to most commercial sales, which commonly extend\ncredit to the buyer\n\n[0m[1mRule: Restatement (Second) § 226 classification of conditions[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 226, Comments a\nand b, following Corbin, described in the casebook Comment\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The Restatement drops the terms independent\ncovenant, concurrent condition, condition precedent, and\ncondition subsequent, and divides conditions into express\nconditions, implied-in-fact conditions (inferred from the\nparties' conduct), and constructive conditions (created by a\ncourt for reasons of justice)\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Where does a condition come from: the parties'\nwords, their conduct, or the court?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Classification by source:\n    1. Express agreement\n    2. Inference from conduct\n    3. Judicial construction for reasons of justice\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This classification is what distinguishes Jacob &\nYoungs (a constructive condition) from the express-condition\ncases in the first part of the chapter\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Except for terminology, the Restatement continues\nthe rules developed by Lord Mansfield and later English and\nAmerican cases\n\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\n[0m[1mJacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent[0m\nJacob & Youngs, Inc. was a building contractor, and it is the\nplaintiff. Kent was the owner who hired it to build a country\nresidence; the casebook pictures the house with the caption \"The\nGeorge Edward Kent estate, circa 1906.\" Kent is the defendant.\nThe opinion does not say where the house was. It describes it\nonly as a \"country residence,\" and the case was litigated in the\nNew York courts. The project cost more than \$77,000.\nConstruction stopped in June 1914, and Kent moved in then.\nAccording to Richard Danzig's research, described in the notes,\nKent moved in after substantial construction delays.\nThe contract's plumbing specifications required that \"all\nwrought-iron pipe must be well galvanized, lap welded pipe of\nthe grade known as 'standard pipe' of Reading manufacture.\"\nArticle IX of the contract said the contractor would be paid\n\"only upon certificates of the Architect.\" Danzig's research\nexplains why a brand name might matter. At the time, wrought\niron pipe cost about 30 percent more than steel pipe, and its\nmakers claimed it saved money through durability and low\nmaintenance. Several companies made wrought iron pipe, and the\nevidence indicates all of it was the same quality. But wrought\niron makers warned buyers to name a specific manufacturer so\nthey would not receive \"wrought pipe,\" a cheaper product steel\ncompanies made from scrap steel.\nThe contract called for 2,000 to 2,500 feet of pipe. When the\nfirst load of about 1,000 feet arrived, Jacob & Youngs'\nsuperintendent pointed out to the plumbing subcontractor's\nforeman that the specifications required Reading pipe. They\nchecked, and it was Reading pipe. After that, the foreman simply\nleft word at the shop for a certain number of feet of pipe\nwithout naming a manufacturer. The pipe was delivered and\ninstalled without anyone checking it: not the contractor, the\nsubcontractor, Kent's architect, or anyone else. Cardozo notes\nthat the architect did inspect pipe on arrival but missed the\ndiscrepancy. Reading pipe could be told apart from other brands\nonly by the maker's name stamped on it every six or seven feet.\nThe pipe was then sealed inside the walls.\nKent raised no complaint until March 1915, when he learned that\nsome of the pipe was not made by Reading. The architect ordered\nJacob & Youngs to redo the work. Because the plumbing was\nenclosed in the walls, that meant tearing out substantial parts\nof the finished house at great expense. After the architect\nrefused the final certificate, the subcontractor uncovered about\n900 feet of exposed pipe in the basement, cellar, and attic. All\nbut 70 feet of it came from other makers: Cohoes Rolling Mill\nCompany, National Steel Works, South Chester Tubing Company, and\nsome with no maker's mark at all. The rest of the pipe could not\nbe inspected without partly demolishing the building. The\nomission was due to the subcontractor's oversight and\ninattention, not fraud or intent, though the dissent called it\ngross neglect. Jacob & Youngs said the other brands were the\nsame as Reading pipe in quality, appearance, market value, and\ncost.\nDanzig suggests that Kent was probably unhappy with the\ncontractor because of the delays and other mistakes in the work,\nand may have been looking for a reason to hold back the rest of\nthe money. Jacob & Youngs did not touch the pipe. It asked for a\ncertificate that the final payment was due, and the architect\nrefused. Jacob & Youngs then sued Kent for the unpaid balance of\n\$3,483.46.\n[0m[1mKingston v. Preston[0m\nThe defendant, Preston, was a silk merchant. The plaintiff,\nKingston, was his apprentice. The case was decided in England by\nthe Court of King's Bench in 1773. The reading does not give a\nmore specific location. The merchant agreed to sell his business\nto the apprentice. In return, the apprentice promised to pay the\nprice in monthly installments and to give \"good and sufficient\nsecurity,\" to be approved by the merchant, for those payments.\nThe deal fell apart when the merchant did not complete the sale.\nThe merchant said this was because the apprentice never provided\nthe promised security. The apprentice sued the merchant for\nfailing to complete the sale of the business.\n[0m[1mMorton v. Lamb[0m\nThe reading identifies the parties only by role. The plaintiff,\nMorton, was buying corn, and the defendant, Lamb, was the one\nwho was supposed to deliver it. The case was decided in England\nby the Court of King's Bench in 1797. The reading gives no other\ndetails about date, place, or the parties' circumstances. The\ncorn was never delivered, and the buyer sued for failure to\ndeliver the corn.\n[0m[1mNichols v. Raynbred[0m\nThis was a sale of a cow, decided by the Court of King's Bench\nin England in 1615. The reading describes the seller suing the\nbuyer for the purchase price. It does not say which named party\nwas the seller and which was the buyer, and it gives no other\nfacts about their circumstances or the location. The dispute was\nover whether the seller had to show that the cow had been\ndelivered. The seller sued the buyer for the price of the cow.\n[0m[1mRoudis v. Hubbard[0m\nThe plaintiffs, Roudis and others, were homeowners who hired the\ndefendant, Hubbard, to build their new home. The case was\ndecided in New York in 1991. The reading does not give the date\nof construction or a more specific location. The contract said\nthat if the finished house substantially complied with the plans\nand specifications, the owners' only remedy would be\nmarket-value damages. The builder apparently left out the\nstyrofoam insulation and footing drains on purpose, because he\nthought they were \"unnecessary.\" The owners sued the builder for\ndamages for failing to install them.\n[0m[1mFlynn Builders, L.C. v. Lande[0m\nFlynn Builders, L.C. was the contractor building a house, and\nLande was the owner. The case was decided in Iowa in 2012. The\nreading does not give the date of construction or a more\nspecific location, and it does not say which party started the\nlawsuit. The contract price was about \$260,000. The contractor\nfinished about 80 to 85 percent of the work. It left undone the\ndoors, cabinets, drywall, flooring, painting, concrete work, and\nheating and air conditioning, which were estimated to take 5 to\n7 more weeks. The reading also indicates that the owner had\nearlier failed to make payments when they were due. The parties\nended up litigating whether the contractor had substantially\nperformed.\n\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\nCases cited only for a single proposition (for example, Spence\nv. Ham, Nolan v. Whitney, Thomas Haverty Co. v. Jones, Hull v.\nGiesler, Nature's Plus, Viacom, Lewis Elec. Co. v. Miller,\nKreyer v. Driscoll, Carrig v. Gilbert-Varker Corp., and First\nNat'l Bank of Omaha v. Centennial Park) appear under the rules\nthey support in Section 1.\n[0m[1mCase Name: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The plaintiff contractor built a country residence for the\n    defendant owner at a cost of more than \$77,000 and sued for\n    the unpaid balance of \$3,483.46\n    - The specifications required wrought-iron pipe \"of Reading\n    manufacture,\" and the contract (Article IX) made payment due\n    \"only upon certificates of the Architect\"\n    - Construction ended in June 1914 and Kent moved in; he made\n    no complaint of defective performance until March 1915\n    - Through the plumbing subcontractor's oversight and\n    inattention, much of the pipe was made by other\n    manufacturers; of about 900 exposed feet, all but 70 were\n    non-Reading\n    - The brands differed only in the maker's name stamped every\n    six to seven feet; even Kent's architect, who inspected the\n    pipe on arrival, missed it\n    - The pipe was enclosed in the walls, so replacing it meant\n    demolishing substantial parts of the finished house at great\n    expense\n    - The architect ordered the work redone; the contractor left\n    it alone and asked for the final certificate, which was\n    refused\n    - The contractor offered to prove the substituted pipe was\n    the same as Reading pipe in quality, appearance, market\n    value, and cost, but the evidence was excluded\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Supreme Court of New York, Trial Term (New York's trial\n    court of general jurisdiction, not its highest court):\n    excluded the contractor's evidence that the substituted pipe\n    was equivalent to Reading pipe and entered judgment for\n    Defendant Kent on a directed verdict\n    - Appeal: Plaintiff-Appellant Jacob & Youngs, Inc. sought\n    review in the Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of New\n    York on the grounds that the trial court erred in excluding\n    its evidence that the deviation was insignificant and in\n    directing a verdict for the defendant\n    - Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of New York\n    (intermediate appellate court): reversed the directed\n    verdict and granted a new trial\n    - Appeal: Defendant-Appellant Kent sought review in the New\n    York Court of Appeals, on a stipulation permitting judgment\n    absolute if the order were affirmed, on the grounds that the\n    trial court correctly excluded the evidence and directed a\n    verdict because the contractor had not performed the\n    contract's requirement of Reading pipe\n    - New York Court of Appeals (New York's highest court): Kent\n    (defendant below) is the appellant; Jacob & Youngs, Inc.\n    (plaintiff below) is the appellee\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a builder whose innocent and trivial deviation\nfrom the specifications (installing non-Reading pipe of equal\nquality) left the owner with substantially what he bargained for\nmay recover the contract balance, subject to an allowance for\nthe defect, and whether that allowance is measured by\nreplacement cost or by difference in value?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The New York Court of Appeals held:\n    - The excluded evidence would have supported an inference\n    that the defect was insignificant in relation to the project\n    - An omission \"both trivial and innocent\" does not breach a\n    condition so as to cause forfeiture, but is atoned for by an\n    allowance for the resulting damage\n    - Because replacement cost here would be grossly\n    disproportionate to the benefit, the allowance is the\n    difference in value, \"either nominal or nothing,\" not the\n    cost of replacement\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Restatement (Second) § 237 substantial performance: Each\n    party's duty is implicitly conditioned on no uncured\n    material failure by the other, so an immaterial deviation\n    leads to damages, not forfeiture\n    - Dependent and independent promises: Some promises are\n    dependent (conditions) when the departure is substantial but\n    independent and collateral when the departure is\n    insignificant, a classification based on justice and\n    presumable intention\n    - Materiality as a question of degree: Whether a deviation\n    is trivial or important cannot be settled by formula and\n    goes to the trier of fact when in doubt, or to the judges\n    when the inferences are certain\n    - Willful transgressor bar: A willful transgressor must\n    accept the penalty, while an unintentional and trivial\n    default may be excused if the defaulting party offers\n    atonement\n    - Measure of damages for defective performance: Cost of\n    replacement is the ordinary measure, but difference in value\n    applies when the cost of completion is grossly and unfairly\n    out of proportion to the good to be attained\n    - Strict performance view: The dissent would have held that\n    the owner is entitled to exactly what he contracted for,\n    whatever his reason, and that a builder who departs from the\n    specifications cannot demand payment\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The New York Court of Appeals (Cardozo, J.)\nreasoned:\n    - The courts never say that less than full performance\n    fulfills one's duty, but a trivial and innocent omis",
        "sion\n    will sometimes be atoned for by damages rather than treated\n    as the breach of a condition followed by forfeiture\n    - Whether a promise is a condition depends \"partly of\n    justice and partly of presumable intention\"; promises cannot\n    be treated as dependent \"to the extent of their uttermost\n    minutiae\" without sacrificing justice and distorting\n    intention\n    - Forfeiture is especially harsh when the work is \"incapable\n    of surrender because united to the land,\" unlike chattels\n    that can be returned\n    - Intention is presumed to \"hold in contemplation the\n    reasonable and probable\"; there will be \"no assumption of a\n    purpose to visit venial faults with oppressive retribution\"\n    - The court chose \"equity and fairness\" over \"symmetry and\n    logic,\" acknowledging that the lines are \"wavering and\n    blurred\" and that a stricter standard would offer more\n    certainty\n    - There is no \"general license\" to substitute something\n    \"just as good,\" and pervasive deviations frustrating the\n    contract's purpose are never tolerated, but this deviation\n    was innocent and appeared trivial\n    - Parties may by \"apt and certain words\" make every term a\n    condition, but the law is slow to imply that where the\n    default is minor compared with the forfeiture\n    - Replacement would require great demolition; Kent did not\n    limit his demand to exposed sections that might have been\n    replaced at moderate expense; the allowance must therefore\n    be the difference in value, shaped as \"an instrument of\n    justice\"\n    - The contractor never got to prove the amount of the\n    allowance because the trial court excluded its evidence of\n    insubstantiality, but an offer of proof aimed at difference\n    in value would not necessarily have been defective\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m:\n    - The non-Reading pipe was the same in quality, appearance,\n    market value, and cost, so it was \"the same thing, though\n    manufactured in another place\"\n    - The omission was neither fraudulent nor willful but\n    resulted from the subcontractor's oversight, and even Kent's\n    architect missed it\n    - Replacing the pipe would require demolishing substantial\n    parts of the completed structure, so it was entitled to the\n    final certificate and the unpaid balance\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m:\n    - The contract expressly required Reading pipe, the\n    contractor failed to supply it, and the architect properly\n    withheld the certificate and ordered the work redone\n    - (As developed in the dissent) only about two-fifths of the\n    pipe conformed, the failure was intentional or grossly\n    negligent, and the contractor offered no explanation and no\n    proof of the cost of compliance where compliance was\n    possible\n    - (As developed in the dissent) the owner had a right to get\n    what he contracted for, even if his reason was \"a mere\n    whim,\" so evidence that the pipe was \"just as good\" was\n    irrelevant and the directed verdict was correct\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The New York Court of Appeals affirmed the\nAppellate Division's order and directed judgment absolute for\nthe plaintiff on the stipulation, with costs in all courts, by a\n4-3 vote (Hiscock, C.J., and Hogan and Crane, JJ., joined\nCardozo; Pound and Andrews, JJ., joined McLaughlin's dissent)\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m:\n    - This is the leading case for the substantial performance\n    doctrine, which the casebook frames as part of the doctrine\n    of constructive conditions\n    - It shows the difference between whether the other party\n    must perform (materiality) and how much the defect is worth\n    (damages)\n    - It introduces the cost-of-completion versus\n    diminution-in-value choice, which Chapter 10 returns to (not\n    covered in the attached material)\n    - It shows the role of good faith and willfulness, later\n    softened by Restatement (Second) § 241(e) and applied\n    strictly in Roudis v. Hubbard\n    - The notes show it could have been decided as an\n    express-condition case because of the\n    architect's-certificate clause, and that the result might\n    differ outside New York\n    - Danzig's background research suggests the \"Reading\" term\n    was really a quality proxy and that Kent may have been\n    looking for a reason to withhold payment, which supports\n    Cardozo's result\n    - Be ready to answer Note 4's question: does the dissent\n    reject the doctrine, or only its application to these facts?\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Kingston v. Preston[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The defendant, a silk merchant, agreed to sell his\n    business to the plaintiff, his apprentice\n    - The apprentice promised to pay the price in monthly\n    installments and to provide \"good and sufficient security\"\n    approved by the merchant\n    - The apprentice sued, alleging the merchant failed to\n    complete the sale\n    - The merchant pleaded that the apprentice never provided\n    the promised security\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Court of King's Bench: the apprentice-buyer sued the\n    silk-merchant seller for failing to complete the sale of the\n    business, and the case is before the court on the\n    apprentice's demurrer to the seller's plea that the\n    apprentice failed to provide the promised security\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether the buyer's promise to provide security for the\nprice was independent of the seller's promise to transfer the\nbusiness, or instead a condition precedent to the seller's duty?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Court of King's Bench (Lord Mansfield) held that\nthe buyer's giving of security was \"clearly a condition\nprecedent\" to the seller's duty to transfer the business\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Mansfield's three kinds of covenants: Covenants are\nmutual and independent, dependent conditions, or mutual\nconditions to be performed at the same time, and which kind a\ncovenant is \"was to be collected from the evident sense and\nmeaning of the parties\"\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Court of King's Bench reasoned:\n    - Covenants fall into three types: independent, dependent\n    and conditional, and concurrent\n    - Dependency is determined by the evident sense and meaning\n    of the parties\n    - \"The essence of the agreement\" was that the seller would\n    not rely on the buyer's personal credit, but would have good\n    security before giving up his stock and business\n    - It \"would be the greatest injustice\" to let the buyer win\n    without having provided the security\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The covenants were\nindependent, so the seller's defense that the buyer failed to\nprovide security was no answer to the buyer's suit\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The buyer's failure to\nprovide the promised security justified the seller's refusal to\ncomplete the sale\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The Court of King's Bench gave judgment for the\ndefendant seller\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m:\n    - Conventionally seen as ending the rule of independent\n    promises and founding the modern doctrine of constructive\n    conditions\n    - It shows that order of performance follows the parties'\n    presumed intent and the risk the deal was structured to\n    avoid (here, extending unsecured credit)\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Morton v. Lamb[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The plaintiff sued for the defendant's failure to deliver\n    corn\n    - The plaintiff's declaration did not allege that he was\n    ready to pay for the corn\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Court of King's Bench: the plaintiff sued the defendant\n    for failure to deliver corn, and the case is before the\n    court on the defendant's motion in arrest of judgment\n    arguing that the plaintiff had not declared he was ready to\n    pay for the corn\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a plaintiff suing for nondelivery under a\ncontract whose performances can be rendered simultaneously must\nallege that he performed, or was ready to perform, his own\nobligation?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Court of King's Bench held that when two\nperformances can be rendered at the same time, they are presumed\n(absent express agreement otherwise) to be due simultaneously,\nso the plaintiff must declare that he performed or at least was\nready to perform\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Restatement (Second) § 234(1) concurrent conditions:\n    Performances that can be rendered at the same time are due\n    simultaneously, and each party's readiness to perform is a\n    concurrent condition of the other's duty\n    - Restatement (Second) § 234(2) longer performance first:\n    When performances cannot be rendered at the same time, the\n    longer performance must be rendered before the shorter one\n    is due\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Court of King's Bench reasoned:\n    - Presuming simultaneous performance means neither party has\n    to extend credit to the other\n    - In contracts such as construction and service contracts,\n    performances cannot be simultaneous, so the longer\n    performance (services) is a condition precedent to the\n    shorter one (payment)\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthe plaintiff's arguments\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The judgment should be\narrested because the plaintiff did not declare that he was ready\nto pay for the corn\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The reading does not state the formal disposition\nof the Court of King's Bench; it states only the rule the court\nannounced\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m:\n    - This is the source of the concurrent-condition and\n    order-of-performance rules now codified in Restatement\n    (Second) § 234 and reflected in UCC §§ 2-507 and 2-511\n    - It explains why, in Jacob & Youngs, the builder's work\n    came before the owner's duty to pay\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Nichols v. Raynbred[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - A seller sued a buyer for the purchase price of a cow\n    - The seller did not plead that the cow had been delivered\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Court of King's Bench: the seller sued the buyer for the\n    purchase price of a cow, and the case is before the court on\n    whether the seller's pleading is sufficient without alleging\n    delivery of the cow\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a seller suing for the price must plead that he\ndelivered the goods, or whether the parties' promises are\nindependent?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Court of King's Bench held that the seller did\nnot have to plead delivery, because the buyer's and seller's\npromises were \"independent\"\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Independent covenants: Mutual promises in a bilateral\ncontract are independent, so one party's nonperformance is no\ndefense to the other's suit and must be pursued in a separate\naction\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Court of King's Bench treated the promises to\npay and to deliver as independent, so the buyer could bring his\nown action for nondelivery but could not use nondelivery as a\ndefense to the seller's action for the price\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthe seller's arguments beyond the sufficiency of the seller's\npleading without an allegation of delivery\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthe buyer's arguments, though the issue implies the buyer argued\ndelivery had to be pleaded\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The reading does not state the formal disposition\nbeyond the holding that the seller need not plead delivery\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: This is the casebook's example of the early\nindependent-promises rule that Kingston v. Preston and Morton v.\nLamb later displaced; it shows why constructive conditions were\nneeded to reach just results\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Roudis v. Hubbard[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The plaintiff homeowners sought damages for the defendant\n    builder's failure to install styrofoam insulation and\n    footing drains in their new home\n    - The contract provided that if the finished product\n    substantially complied with the plans and specifications,\n    the owners' only remedy would be market-value damages\n    - The builder apparently left out the drains and insulation\n    on purpose because he thought them \"unnecessary\"\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - New York trial court: entered judgment for the plaintiff\n    homeowners on their claim for damages for the omitted\n    insulation and footing drains\n    - Appeal: Defendant-Appellant Hubbard sought review in the\n    Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of New York on the\n    grounds that, because the defects did not diminish the\n    house's value and the contract limited the owners to\n    market-value damages upon substantial compliance, the owners\n    suffered no compensable damages\n    - Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of New York\n    (intermediate appellate court): Hubbard (defendant below) is\n    the appellant; Roudis (plaintiffs below) is the appellee\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a builder who intentionally omitted specified\nwork may limit the owners to diminution-in-value damages, even\nunder a contract making market-value damages the sole remedy\nupon substantial compliance?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Appellate Division held that because the builder\nintentionally omitted the required work, the proper remedy was\nthe cost of completion, since diminution in value applies only\nwhere the breach was unintentional and constituted substantial\nperformance in good faith\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Diminution in value limited to good-faith breach: Even\nif a contractor has substantially performed, diminution in value\nshould be applied only where the breach was unintentional and\nconstituted substantial performance in good faith\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Appellate Division relied on Jacob & Youngs and\nits distinction between the innocent and the willful\ntransgressor, reasoning that a builder who deliberately decides\nrequired work is \"unnecessary\" cannot use the\ndiminution-in-value measure\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The owners argued they were\nentitled to damages for the builder's failure to install the\ninsulation and footing drains the contract called for\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: Because the defects did not\ndiminish the house's value, and the contract made market-value\ndamages the sole remedy upon substantial compliance, the owners\nsuffered no compensable damages\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The Appellate Division affirmed the trial court's\njudgment for the plaintiffs\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: It shows Jacob & Youngs' willfulness limit\noperating at the damages stage; contrast it with Restatement\n(Second) § 241(e), which treats motive only as a factor\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Flynn Builders, L.C. v. Lande[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: CB pp 850-863\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Contracts\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - Flynn Builders, the contractor, completed about 80 to 85\n    percent of a house under a contract priced at about \$260,000\n    - The unfinished work included doors, cabinets, drywall,\n    flooring, painting, concrete work, and heating and air\n    conditioning, estimated to take 5 to 7 weeks\n    - The owner had earlier failed to make payments when due,\n    which became important on remand\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Iowa trial court: the opinion does not state the ruling or\n    procedural vehicle\n    - The opinion does not state the movement type, the moving\n    party, or the alleged error\n    - Iowa Supreme Court: the opinion does not state which party\n    is the appellant and which is the appellee\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a contractor who completed about 80 to 85\npercent of the work on a house, leaving work that affected its\nhabitability, substantially performed?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The Iowa Supreme Court held that the contractor had\nnot substantially performed, because the unfinished work\n\"materially affected the habitability of the house\"\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Substantial performance standard: Performance is\n    substantial only if the variance does not impair the\n    structure as a whole and the owner still gets essentially\n    what he contracted for, and no mathematical percentage rule\n    controls\n    - Prior nonpayment excusing the contractor's performance: A\n    contractor's failure to substantially perform may be excused\n    by the owner's earlier failure to make payments when due\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The Iowa Supreme Court reasoned that no\nmathematical rule can be stated about the percentage of\ncompletion required, and that the nature of the unfinished work,\nwhich affected habitability, ruled out a finding of substantial\nperformance\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthe parties' arguments, though the issue implies the contractor\nargued its performance was substantial\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthe parties' arguments, though the issue implies the owner\nargued the unfinished work defeated substantial performance\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The Iowa Supreme Court held there was no\nsubstantial performance and the case went back to the trial\ncourt on remand; the trial court then found the contractor's\nfailure excused by the owner's earlier failure to pay, and the\nIowa Court of Appeals affirmed that ruling in an unpublished\ntable decision\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m:\n    - Percentage of completion is not decisive; what matters is\n    the qualitative effect of the shortfall, such as\n    habitability\n    - The remand shows that the other party's own prior breach\n    can excuse a failure to substantially perform\n\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mMaterial breach[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An uncured failure of performance significant\nenough that it is a failure of the constructive condition to the\nother party's duty, so the other party may withhold its own\nperformance; Hull v. Giesler describes it as one that \"touches\nfundamental purpose of contract\" and \"defeats the purpose of the\nparties\"\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the title concept of this section; whether\na breach is material decides whether the other party must still\nperform\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: An immaterial breach gives only a right to\ndamages; a material breach also suspends or discharges the other\nparty's duty\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Flynn Builders, L.C. v.\nLande\n\n[0m[1mSubstantial performance[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Performance whose departures from the contract are\nimmaterial, so the constructive condition to the other party's\nduty is satisfied, though the performing party remains liable\nfor damages for the deviations\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It let Jacob & Youngs recover the balance\ndespite the non-Reading pipe; the casebook calls Jacob & Youngs\nthe leading case adopting the principle\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is not full performance (\"the courts never\nsay\" less than full performance fulfills the duty); it is the\nopposite of material breach; it is not available under Cardozo's\nview to a willful transgressor\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Flynn Builders, L.C. v.\nLande; Roudis v. Hubbard\n\n[0m[1mConstructive condition[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A condition created by a court \"for reasons of\njustice\" to determine the consequences of breach when the\nparties have not spelled them out, making one party's duty\ndependent on the other's performance\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the doctrinal framework of the reading;\nsubstantial performance is \"one aspect\" of it; it may arguably\nreflect what the parties would have agreed had they bargained\nover the matter\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: An express condition comes from the parties'\nagreement a",
        "nd is generally strictly enforced; an implied-in-fact\ncondition is inferred from the parties' conduct; a constructive\ncondition is imposed by the court\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Kingston v. Preston;\nMorton v. Lamb\n\n[0m[1mExpress condition[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A condition resulting from the parties' agreement,\nso that a duty does not arise, absent excuse, unless and until\nthe specified event occurs\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Cardozo noted that parties may \"by apt and\ncertain words\" make every term a condition; Note 3 shows that\nthe architect's-certificate clause could have made Jacob &\nYoungs an express-condition case\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Under the majority view, an express condition is\nstrictly enforced unless excused (fraud, bad faith, waiver),\nwhile a constructive condition is satisfied by substantial\nperformance\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent\n\n[0m[1mImplied-in-fact condition[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Under Restatement (Second) § 226, a condition\ninferred from the conduct of the parties\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is one of the Restatement's three categories\nof conditions, following Corbin\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is inferred from conduct, unlike express\nconditions (stated in words) and constructive conditions\n(imposed by the court for justice)\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: None (general terminology)\n\n[0m[1mDependent promise[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A promise whose performance is a condition of the\nother party's duty, so the other party need not perform until it\nis performed\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Cardozo used dependent versus independent\npromises to explain substantial performance; some promises are\ndependent only when the departure is substantial\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is the opposite of an independent promise;\nMansfield's \"conditions and dependent\" covenants require prior\nperformance by one side\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Kingston v. Preston\n\n[0m[1mIndependent promise[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A promise whose breach gives the other party a\ndamages claim but does not excuse that party's own performance\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was the early seventeenth-century English\ndefault for bilateral contracts; Cardozo said an insignificant\ndeparture makes an otherwise dependent promise \"independent and\ncollateral\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A breach of an independent promise must be\npursued in a separate action rather than raised as a defense,\nunlike a breach of a dependent promise\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Nichols v. Raynbred; Kingston v. Preston; Jacob &\nYoungs, Inc. v. Kent\n\n[0m[1mConcurrent condition[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Where performances can be rendered at the same\ntime, each party's readiness and tender of performance is a\ncondition of the other party's duty\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It comes from Morton v. Lamb and Mansfield's\nthird category; it is codified as the default rule in\nRestatement (Second) § 234(1) and reflected in UCC §§ 2-507 and\n2-511\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Unlike a condition precedent, neither party must\nperform first; each must only show tender to sue; the\nRestatement no longer uses the term\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Morton v. Lamb; Kingston v. Preston\n\n[0m[1mCondition precedent[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A performance or event that must occur before the\nother party's duty to perform arises\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: In Kingston v. Preston, the buyer's provision of\nsecurity was a condition precedent; under Morton v. Lamb, the\nlonger performance, such as services, is a condition precedent\nto payment\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It differs from a concurrent condition\n(simultaneous exchange); the Restatement (Second) abandons the\nterm in favor of its express, implied-in-fact, and constructive\ncategories\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Kingston v. Preston; Morton v. Lamb\n\n[0m[1mForfeiture[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The loss of the right to the other party's return\nperformance (such as payment) because a condition was not met,\neven though the performing party has conferred substantial value\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Cardozo's opinion is driven by avoiding\n\"oppressive retribution\" for \"venial faults,\" especially where\nthe work is attached to land and cannot be returned\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Forfeiture follows from failure of a condition;\nan immaterial breach leads only to an allowance for damages\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent\n\n[0m[1mWillful transgressor[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Cardozo's term for a party whose deviation from\nthe contract was intentional, and who therefore \"must accept the\npenalty of his transgression\" and cannot invoke substantial\nperformance\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It marks the good-faith limit on substantial\nperformance; the dissent argued gross neglect amounted to the\nsame thing\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Restatement (Second) § 241(e), following Corbin,\ntreats willfulness only as a factor, not an automatic bar;\nRoudis v. Hubbard applies willfulness to deny the\ndiminution-in-value measure\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Roudis v. Hubbard\n\n[0m[1mCost of completion[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A damages measure equal to the money needed to fix\nor complete the defective work so it conforms to the contract\n(also called cost of replacement or repair)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the ordinary measure under Jacob & Youngs;\nit was applied in Roudis because the breach was intentional, and\nin Lewis Elec. Co. v. Miller\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It gives way to difference in value when it is\n\"grossly and unfairly out of proportion to the good to be\nattained\"\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Roudis v. Hubbard\n\n[0m[1mDifference in value[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A damages measure equal to the difference between\nthe value of the performance as promised and its value as\nactually rendered (also called diminution in value or\nmarket-value damages)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was the allowance in Jacob & Youngs, \"either\nnominal or nothing,\" because replacement would have required\ndemolition\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is the exception to the cost-of-completion\ndefault, and under Roudis it is available only for\nunintentional, good-faith breaches\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent; Roudis v. Hubbard\n\n[0m[1mQuantum meruit[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Restitutionary recovery of the reasonable value of\nservices rendered, which many courts allow even to a contractor\nthat committed a material breach, less any harm from the breach\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is a fallback recovery when substantial\nperformance fails\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is measured by the value of the services, not\nthe contract price minus damages (the substantial performance\nrecovery)\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: None (general terminology)\n\n[0m[1mDivisible contract[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Under Restatement (Second) § 240, a contract whose\nperformances can be split into corresponding pairs of part\nperformances that may properly be treated as agreed equivalents\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It permits recovery for completed portions\ndespite failing to finish the whole, as in Carrig v.\nGilbert-Varker Corp. (20 of 35 houses)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is a different route to recovery from\nsubstantial performance (which looks at the whole contract) and\nfrom restitution (which looks at reasonable value)\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: None (general terminology)\n\n[0m[1mAgreed equivalents[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Pairs of part performances that it is proper to\ntreat as exchanged for each other, because the part received is\nworth to the recipient roughly the same fraction of what full\nperformance would have been worth\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the second requirement for divisibility,\nprotecting the expectations of a party who received only part of\nthe performance\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Mere mathematical apportionment (the first\nrequirement) is not enough without this equivalence\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: None (general terminology)\n\n[0m[1mTender[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An offer of one's own performance, showing\nreadiness to perform, which satisfies a concurrent condition so\nthat a party may sue the other for nonperformance\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Under Restatement (Second) § 234(1) and UCC §§\n2-507 and 2-511, a buyer or seller must show at least a tender\nto maintain an action for breach\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Tender is less than actual completed performance;\nit is the \"ready, and offered\" showing in Mansfield's third\ncategory\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Morton v. Lamb; Kingston v. Preston\n\n[0m[1mArchitect's certificate[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An architect's certification that work is complete\nor payment is due, which a construction contract may make an\nexpress condition of payment\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Kent's contract (Article IX) conditioned payment\non such certificates, and the refusal of the final certificate\ntriggered the suit\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: In New York an architect cannot properly refuse\nit if the contractor has substantially performed (Nolan v.\nWhitney); in most jurisdictions it is strictly enforced unless\nexcused by fraud, bad faith, or waiver\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Jacob & Youngs, Inc. v. Kent\n"
    )
    val codePoints: IntArray
        get() = textChunks.joinToString("").codePoints().toArray()
}

interface GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String
}

class SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy : GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    override fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String {
        val builder = StringBuilder()
        for (codePoint in codePoints) {
            builder.appendCodePoint(codePoint)
        }
        return builder.toString()
    }
}

class DocumentAssemblyEngine(private val strategy: GlyphAssemblyStrategy) {
    fun render(): String = strategy.assemble(CodePointRepository.codePoints)
}

class ConsoleOutputSink {
    fun writeLine(payload: String) = println(payload)
}

class DocumentCompilerFacade {
    private val engine = DocumentAssemblyEngine(SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy())
    private val sink = ConsoleOutputSink()

    fun execute() {
        sink.writeLine(engine.render())
    }
}

fun main() {
    DocumentCompilerFacade().execute()
}
